# core/inventory-summary - What is loaded, in one screen?
#
# Answers: per notation, how many models arrived, how many semantic graphs their
# concepts occupy, and how many concepts they contain.
#
# Does NOT prove: completeness. A notation absent from this table is a notation
# nothing in this dataset declares - which may mean it was never converted, or
# that a merge dropped it. Only the publishing project's own manifest can tell
# those apart, and linked-archi-connect reports that when it attaches.
#
# Run this instead of core/inventory during orientation on a large dataset.
# core/inventory groups by graph AND type, so it returns graphs x types rows -
# on an estate with 134 named graphs that runs into thousands, truncates, and
# buries the signal it exists to provide. This rolls up to one row per notation,
# which stays readable at any size. Reach for core/inventory once you know which
# notation to look inside.
#
# arch:modelConformsToMetamodel is the reliable notation signal, far more so than
# inspecting element types or parsing IRIs, since the notation segment of an IRI
# is configurable per converter. A model that declares no metamodel appears with
# an unbound notation rather than being dropped - unlabelled is a finding.
#
# THE MODEL IS READ THROUGH THE `model` GRAPH ROLE, and concepts are joined to it
# through {{MEMBERSHIP}}. Both matter, and this template previously did neither:
# it declared the model inside the semantic graph and counted concepts by
# co-location, which is correct only for the pre-1.3 layout. On a 1.3 dataset
# arch:Model lives in graph/model, so the whole query matched nothing and returned
# ZERO ROWS - from the one template the skill tells you to run first, and whose
# job is to tell an empty dataset apart from a dataset with no matches. Observed
# on a 1.4M-quad estate where every notation was in fact present.
#
# Asking for the role and the membership mode instead of hardcoding either is what
# lets this serve both generations. Under the pre-1.3 profile `model` is bound to
# the semantic suffix and membership is co-location, so the rendered query is
# equivalent to what it was before; under 1.3 it reads graph/model and joins on
# the one-hop arch:inModel edge.
#
# Relationships are concepts too, so this count includes them;
# core/discover-relationship-types breaks them out.
#
# Only one OPTIONAL joins concepts, deliberately. Adding a second independent
# OPTIONAL - relationships alongside concepts - would multiply them into a
# concepts x relationships intermediate per graph before aggregation.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?metamodel
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?model) AS ?models)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT {{GRAPH_VAR:semantic}}) AS ?graphs)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?concept) AS ?concepts)
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:model}}
    ?model a {{ROLE:model_class}} .
    OPTIONAL { ?model {{ROLE:conforms_to_metamodel}} ?metamodel }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
      ?concept a {{ROLE:concept_class}} .
      {{MEMBERSHIP:concept}}
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
GROUP BY ?metamodel
ORDER BY DESC(?concepts) ?metamodel
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
